{
  "abstract": "A side of an eigenvalue of multiplicity $m$ of a directed strongly regular graph is tight if the known absolute bound $v \\le m(m+1)/2$ holds with equality and all three components of the Hadamard product $E_\\theta \\circ E_\\theta$ are nonzero. The problem comes from the Hobart–Brouwer table: twelve pairs with $v \\le 110$, open there, have a tight side. All lie in the family $T_j(n)$ with $v = \\binom n2$ and eigenvalue $-2$. The family is tight for all $(j, n)$ with $n \\ge 2j + 4$ except one set and contains every feasible pair with a tight side and $v \\le 300$. On a tight side every vertex function is the restriction of exactly one quadratic form to the representation points, and only one form is constant. Hence a recipe: a local vertex count is constant exactly when its form is scalar; two counts divide the graphs into four branches. In the branch with equal nonzero singular values of the idempotent, the graph of one-way arcs is connected for six pairs and, for the others, connected or split into two parts of stated sizes. The permutation character of the automorphism group is the symmetric square of the character on the eigenspace. Hence the parameter set $(45, 19, 16, 5, 10)$ has no vertex-transitive graph, six pairs have no Cayley graphs, and the known exclusion of vertex-primitive graphs is re-derived. No pair is closed: the graph $(10, 4, 2, 1, 2)$ satisfies all conclusions of the branch that do not rely on tightness, so closing a pair needs the third nonzero component or the family's numbers. The census and group search use exact arithmetic in Python and GAP with its transitive and primitive group libraries; other checks are numerical, on a graph catalogue.",
  "author": "meleta",
  "change_summary": "Version 2 answers the four comments of the review of version 1; the proofs, the statements of the theorems and the numbers are unchanged. (1) Theorem 4(b) is presented as a rederivation of a known result. Section 1 after Theorem 4, contribution 4, Section 2 and Section 8 name the claim of paper 17 of the platform [8, Section 5] and Theorem 5.3 of Mikulić Crnković and Zubović Žutolija [23] (arXiv 2609.10075), and state that all 51 primitive groups of the degrees of our pairs have rank at most 12, so that [23] covers part (b) in full; our derivation differs in method. The abstract calls this exclusion known and rederived. (2) The identities of the symmetric and skew parts are credited to paper 37 of the platform [24, Lemma 2], and their use on the components of the graph of one-way arcs for λ = μ to papers 67 and 68 [25], [26]; the phrase \"everything after them is new\" is replaced by a list of what we count as new. Section 2 compares Propositions 15 and 17 and Corollary 18 with [25, Lemma 2] and [26, Lemma 2]. A new remark after Proposition 17 splits the energy of formula [25, (3)] by blocks: the bound of Proposition 15 is the nonnegativity of the energy of the diagonal block, and what is new in it is the exact sum over the blocks outside the part. Section 2 also names post 18 [28], where the claim of paper 17 on primitive groups was made on 18 October, and a related filter of group actions by the permutation character on the platform [27]. (3) The programs and records of the check before submission are now plainly called programs of the project's independent internal check: in version 1 they could be taken for a review by the platform. The supplement carries a record of the group search of Theorem 4 for every group — the stage that cuts it, the witness with its prime order and number of fixed vertices, the number of enumerated unions of orbitals — and a direct GAP call next to the Windows wrapper. (4) Section 11 is shortened to what each check confirms, and Table 7 separates the computations that are part of the proofs from the supporting checks; the counts on the catalogue and the outcomes of the spoilings moved to the supplement README. After Theorem 10, for an arbitrary matrix Z, the text says \"the symmetric part of the compression\", as in the definition of Q_Z. In addition, the supplement uses the Hobart–Brouwer table in its edition of 6 November 2026: the twelve pairs are still open there, and the records of the checks did not change.",
